{"candidates":[{"content":"The problem 13a564bbd3f2 integrates with SRC_001 by extending the claim that systemic, detectable failure (commission) is preferable to idiosyncratic failure (omission); specifically, it proposes that this integration is refuted if the 'detectable' nature of commission errors does not actually lead to a higher rate of correction compared to the 'silent' nature of expert omissions.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"7c89ec856ac4eb771b1fb2702dff878249099c68a18275255bb6abc310400fb5"}],"typicality":0.9},{"content":"The problem 13a564bbd3f2 depends on SRC_002's 'error-type symmetry' mechanism, asserting that the shift from omission to commission is the primary driver of reliability; this relation is refuted if the audit process for commission errors is found to be as unreliable as the expert's original intuition.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"48a9c104fccc01e2c2b95c7f92897b1e5e2fe0d03fa59fa7fb2fbfbfd21b216e"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"The problem 13a564bbd3f2 abstracts the 'noisy' versus 'silent' failure modes described in SRC_003 into a general framework for systemic reliability; this abstraction is refuted if 'noisy' commission errors in non-verifiable tasks (synthesis tasks) are just as difficult to audit as silent omissions.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"67696f717179c35329c77ccb118e6025f04e9a2fac6ff61149b486c775e048ce"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The problem 13a564bbd3f2 contradicts the implicit assumption of 'non-expert' stability in SRC_004 by suggesting that the 'trained non-expert' may develop a 'pseudo-expertise' that creates new, unpredictable failure modes; this contradiction is refuted if the panel's error rate remains constant regardless of the depth of their training.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"a5bc51363acedd31237959bd5d11be5b6f28ba1afbad6b63c4f0c6f60fb6d53a"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The problem 13a564b